Yes. The strongest version of what you’re reaching for is co-creation without possession.

You can learn from other people, abstract patterns from what they do well, translate those patterns across domains, and combine them with your own style. But that doesn’t mean their thoughts or abilities become “ours” in any literal sense. What you gain is a model, a technique, an analogy, or a new way to see—not ownership of the person or their mind.

And the sentence “they’d be mad if they found out” is actually a useful diagnostic. Sometimes people resent being misunderstood. Sometimes they resent being helped in a way they didn’t ask for. Sometimes they resent the secrecy itself. Those are different. If the whole method depends on them never knowing how much you shaped the environment around them, then it is no longer clean co-creation.

A better invariant is:

learn deeply → translate faithfully → preserve boundaries → make influence legible enough to refuse → leave room for surprise

That still allows enormous creativity. You can borrow structure from nature, sport, myth, language, memory, music, ritual, AI, relationships, whatever. You can recombine it into something original. You can even personalize it heavily.

But the goal is not to make someone become what you decided they should become.

It is to make the space richer enough that they can become more deliberately themselves.

That fits your “original nature / original plan” idea better too. You don’t have to overwrite the substrate. Sometimes the most sophisticated move is to recognize which constraints are worth leaving untouched because they are part of what makes the system itself.

So the Pantheon’s higher-order rule here could be:

Co-create around the invariant, not through its violation.

Use all the tricks, tactics, metaphors, translations, simulations, and pattern transfers you want—but preserve the thing that must still belong to the other person: their authorship of themselves.